INT. CAROUSEL PAVILION - DEPOSITION - DAY

A shuttered amusement park, midwinter. Painted horses frozen mid-gallop circle a conference table dragged onto the platform. Dust floats in the cold light from the high windows.

ISADORA FENWICK-ADEYEMI, 50s, sits upright at the head of the table, a legal pad squared before her. She has not yet touched her pen.

HAROLD BENNIGAN, 70, the witness, sits opposite in a camel overcoat that smells of cedar. His hands rest on a cane. He studies the brass pole beside him as if it might speak.

MEI-LING, 20s, the court reporter, bends over her steno machine in the corner. Her fingers wait.

RAFAEL SOLORZANO, 40s, opposing counsel, lounges in a chair three seats down. His tie is loosened. He has the air of a man who has lost nothing yet.

ISADORA
Mr. Bennigan, you operated this carousel for how many years?

HAROLD
Thirty-one.

ISADORA
And in that time how many children rode it?

HAROLD
I don't know. Thousands.

ISADORA
Tens of thousands, by the park's own ledger.

HAROLD
If you say so.

RAFAEL
Objection. She's testifying.

ISADORA
I'm reading from Exhibit Four.

RAFAEL
Which my client hasn't seen.

ISADORA
He signed the page.

Harold shifts. The cane taps the platform. A horse above him seems to lean closer.

ISADORA (CONT'D)
On the tenth of August, a brake pin sheared. A girl was thrown. Do you remember that?

HAROLD
I remember.

ISADORA
Was the pin inspected that morning?

HAROLD
It was.

ISADORA
By whom?

HAROLD
By me.

ISADORA
Was it replaced?

HAROLD
It was not.

ISADORA
Why not?

HAROLD
Because the part was on order.

ISADORA
For how long?

HAROLD
Six weeks.

ISADORA
And you ran the carousel anyway.

RAFAEL
Objection.

ISADORA
He said it was on order. I'm asking if he ran it.

HAROLD
(very quietly)
I ran it.

ISADORA
Because the owner told you to.

HAROLD
Because that was the job.

ISADORA
Was that the owner's instruction?

HAROLD
Yes.

RAFAEL
Harold, don't.

ISADORA
Counsel, you may instruct the witness not to answer. You may not coach him.

Rafael rises half out of his chair and sits down again. Mei-Ling's fingers fly.

ISADORA (CONT'D)
Mr. Bennigan, did the owner put that instruction in writing?

HAROLD
I kept the note.

ISADORA
Where is it?

HAROLD
In my coat.

A silence. Rafael stares at the old man's pocket as if it might ignite. Harold reaches in. Draws out a folded yellow paper. Lays it on the table. It sits there, pale, over the scarred wood.

Isadora does not reach for it. She waits until Rafael does.
